---
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro'
import Feature from '../../components/Feature.astro'
import Snapshot from '../../components/Snapshot.astro'
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro'
import { FEATURES } from '../../features'
import FeaturePage from '../../layouts/FeaturePage.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../../meta'

export const meta = {
  description: FEATURES.plugins.sentence,
  sources: [
    'docs/extensibility.md',
    'docs/extensibility/runtime.md',
    'docs/extensibility/ui-and-cooperation.md',
    'docs/plugin-map.md',
    'docs/contribution-kinds.md',
    'docs/panes.md',
    'docs/github-integration/surfaces.md',
    'docs/plugins/agent-install.md',
    'docs/security/node-plugin-security.md',
    'docs/security/plugin-bundles.md',
    'docs/tui/plugin-losses.md',
    'docs/first-party-plugins/terminal.md',
  ],
  checked: '663359ff7',
} satisfies PageMeta

const guides = [
  ['/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-a-pane/', 'Add a pane', 'A view in the task’s row of panes.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-a-source-to-the-left-rail/', 'Add a source to the left rail', 'A list to browse, with items you can open.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-commands-and-keybindings/', 'Add commands and keybindings', 'Rows in the command palette, and keys for them.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-a-settings-page/', 'Add a settings page', 'A page of its own in Settings.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/node/give-agents-tools/', 'Give agents tools', 'Tools your agent sessions can call.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/node/add-a-harness/', 'Add a harness', 'Another agent program for acorn to run.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/node/add-a-workflow-step/', 'Add a workflow step', 'A step kind with its own form.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/node/connect-a-service/', 'Connect a service', 'An account acorn holds the credential for.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/node/add-a-data-source/', 'Add a data source', 'Typed records for dashboards and workflows.'],
  ['/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-a-theme-or-a-style-pack/', 'Add a theme or a style pack', 'Colors, or a whole look.'],
]
---

<FeaturePage feature="plugins" meta={meta} related={['desktop-and-terminal', 'security', 'task-tools']} docs={{ href: '/docs/build-plugins/start/build-your-first-plugin/', label: 'Build your first plugin' }}>
  <Specimen slot="picture" id="issues-pane" both />

  <Feature number={1} title="Most of acorn is plugins" href="/docs/build-plugins/start/how-plugins-work/" link="How plugins work">
    <p>
      Git, GitHub, the Agent pane, the editor, and workflows are all plugins. Database, HTTP, Linear, and Rollbar ship
      with acorn as installed packages, and run through the same loader, sandbox, and permission checks as a plugin
      you install yourself.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={2} title="Drawn with acorn's own components" href="/docs/build-plugins/start/choose-how-your-plugin-draws/" link="Choose how your plugin draws">
    <p>
      A plugin's pane code runs in a worker and sends a tree of acorn's components, which the app draws. So the pane
      gets the app's keys, focus, screen reader labels, and themes, and the terminal client draws it too. A plugin
      that needs its own pixels can draw a web page in a frame instead, on the desktop only.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={3} title="What a plugin can add" href="/docs/build-plugins/reference/contributions/" link="Contributions">
    <p>Each of these is an entry in the plugin's manifest, and each has a guide.</p>
    <ul class="cards">
      {
        guides.map(([href, title, text]) => (
          <li>
            <Card title={title} href={href}>
              <p>{text}</p>
            </Card>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={4} title="Plugins extending each other" href="/docs/build-plugins/ui/add-to-another-plugins-view/" link="Add to another plugin's view">
    <p>
      A plugin can open places in its own view for others to fill. GitHub takes marks on the lines of a pull
      request's diff and badges on its overview, and the Context pane takes a section from any plugin. Each
      contribution draws on both clients, from the same code.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={5} title="Permissions you accept on each device" href="/docs/security/plugin-trust-and-permissions/" link="Plugin trust and permissions">
    <p>
      An installed plugin's manifest lists what it needs, and its Node half runs in a worker that gets only those
      grants. Each device decides whether to run a plugin's exact bytes, and an update asks again with what changed.
      acorn doesn't review or sign plugins. The decision is yours, on each device.
    </p>
    <Snapshot slot="picture" id="permissions" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={6} title="Ask your agent to write one" href="/docs/build-plugins/start/have-your-agent-build-it/" link="Have your agent build it">
    <p>
      acorn's agents read the plugin vocabulary from your Node with the <code>plugin_authoring</code> tool. When the
      plugin is ready, the agent asks to install it, and nothing is installed until you approve it.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Fragment slot="terminal">
    <p>
      A plugin's tree draws in the terminal client with no extra code, and a graph becomes an indented list. What
      the terminal can't draw is a frame: a plugin's own web page shows as one line that points at the desktop app.
      The terminal client asks the same trust question before it runs a plugin.{' '}
      <a href="/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/">Desktop and terminal</a>
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FeaturePage>
